Z||000000||Voyd||11-03-1999||10:23 AM||||alright, i am having a problem which ive described in the thread <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001248.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001248.html</A>  theogre said it might be a TCC lockup. ok, so i took off the cover and i could see down into the tranny. i was told to unplug the electrical connector. all i see is a big fat black wire in the front of the tranny going up further than i can see. is this the TCC i am supposed to unplug? just wanna make sure i dont screw anything up.<P>and when it is unplugged, can i just start the car and idle for a bit without worrying?<P>oh yeah ,and also, if doing this procedure doesnt work, is it possible it might be due to bad sensors? i checked the trouble codes, and i was returned <BR>Code 33 - manifold Air Pressure Sensor <BR>Code 44 - Lean Exhaust (replace Oxygen sensor)<P>thanks for all help, i really appreciate it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||11-03-1999||12:34 PM||||I don't know what wires to unplug, never had an Auto Fiero.  All the TCC does is "locks" the transmission so it can no longer slip.  Kinda' like on a stick, the clutch can slip while your letting it out, but once the pedal is completely released the disk is "locked" to the flywheel. Similar to an automatic.<BR>If you ever go on the freeway and lightly tap the brakes while holding the throttle steady, you'll feel the TCC "unlock" for a moment.  Having the TCC unhooked allows for higher transmission temps, due to the constant slipping so I wouldn't leave it unhooked for an extended period of time, but I believe it's safe to unhook it, just to see if it is the cause of your problems.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-03-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-03-1999||01:17 PM||||You didn't need to remove the cover. the plug for the TCC solinoid is on the front of the tranny. It is external.<P>You can drive the car normally w/o the TCC lockup. You have to drive it to test it....<P>In this case the brake test isn't always enough. Plus it's hard to see the RPM change on some tachs. The RPM's usually only change about 200. You usually need to drive it a bit w/o the tcc conected.<P>likely tho the TCC lock isn't your problem if you got codes. could be but not likely. the car usually just dies at traffic lights.<P>Those codes together are very likely vacuum leaks, broken disconnected or incorrectly connected vacuum lines. Could be a bad MAP sensor.||||reg||1
